<div class="prose"><p>Once a meaning is established, the system needs ways to carry it into work. An accepted record format states which information a record must contain. Connection rules state which records may be linked and under which requirements. Policies and explicit permissions state what an agent may do, for which task and under whose responsibility.</p><p>Each governs a particular question through its own owner and scope. A definition can govern the meaning used in an assessment while a policy governs the actions permitted during that assessment. Acceptance identifies the governing version and purpose; evidence and operational records support claims about what actually happened.</p></div>
<div class="authority-strips" role="group" aria-label="Different authority surfaces">
<div><strong>Definition</strong><span>The meaning of a term—for example, which customer rows qualify for inclusion.</span></div>
<div><strong>Artifact type</strong><span>A required record format—for example, the fields needed to describe an export capability.</span></div>
<div><strong>Relation kind</strong><span>A rule for a connection—for example, which kinds of records an experiment may evaluate, with which details.</span></div>
<div><strong>Policy + authority scope</strong><span>The applicable rules, who may authorize actions and the limits of that permission.</span></div>
</div>
<details class="facet-details"><summary>Seven details to keep separate when reading a record</summary>
<p class="small">Authority, provenance and lifecycle are three ways to examine a record. The Governance-Axis Independence Invariant explicitly separates kind, status, evidence confidence, commitment confidence, provenance and owner when they matter to a decision. The wider model also tracks residue. Strong evidence does not automatically mean that an owner has committed to a proposal.</p>
<div class="facet-list"><div><strong>Kind</strong><p>What is this: a definition, task, observation or another kind of record?</p></div><div><strong>Status</strong><p>Is it proposed, accepted or in another state defined for that kind?</p></div><div><strong>Evidence confidence</strong><p>How well does the evidence support this particular claim?</p></div><div><strong>Commitment confidence</strong><p>How strongly does the owner intend to proceed?</p></div><div><strong>Provenance</strong><p>Which sources, people, software versions and transformations produced it?</p></div><div><strong>Owner</strong><p>Who is responsible for this record and the decisions within its scope?</p></div><div><strong>Residue</strong><p>What remains unresolved, missing or not preserved?</p></div></div>
</details>
{{SOURCES_AUTHORITY}}
